# The container needs requirements.txt + the app/ package. Nothing else.
#
# This list is also what the CDK Docker image asset STAGES, and the staged bytes
# are what the asset hash is computed from — so anything that leaks in here causes
# a pointless image rebuild and redeploy every time it changes.
#
# Patterns are matched with dockerignore semantics, where a bare `__pycache__/`
# matches only at the ROOT. Nested caches need `**/` — without it, importing the
# app or running the test suite writes app/**/__pycache__, those files get staged,
# and the asset hash changes even though no source did.
**/__pycache__/
**/*.py[cod]
.venv/
venv/

# Secrets, never in an image layer.
.env
*.env
.creds.env

# Documentation.
*.md
docs/

# Deployed separately, not needed inside the orchestrator container:
#   bff/, kb_lambda/, app/tools/    -> their own Lambda zips
#   web/                            -> static assets on S3/CloudFront
#   kb_docs/                        -> uploaded to S3 and ingested by the KB
#
# app/tools/ is the one that needs saying out loud, because it sits UNDER app/ and it
# is the only CODE there that does not ship. It holds the source of the functions behind
# `type: "lambda"` tools, which the IaC zips and deploys on their own — the
# orchestrator calls them through the Gateway and never imports them.
terraform/
cdk/
cdk.out/
node_modules/
bff/
kb_lambda/
app/tools/
web/
kb_docs/

# Developer tooling. The config-plane test suite runs on a developer machine or in
# CI, never in the container; excluded so editing a test cannot invalidate the
# image layer cache and trigger a rebuild + redeploy.
tests/
pytest.ini
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/
requirements-dev.txt
format_workflow.py
build_schema.py
# EDITOR-FACING, not runtime. app/keys.json describes which keys workflow.json may
# contain and app/workflow.schema.json is generated from it for your editor — together
# ~40 KB of documentation that nothing in the container reads (the runtime reads
# workflow.json, vocabulary.json and defaults.json, which DO ship). Excluded for the same
# reason tests/ is: editing a doc string should not invalidate the image layer and trigger
# a rebuild and redeploy.
#
# app/defaults.json exists BECAUSE of this exclusion. keys.json is where every key's
# default is authored, but the running code needs those values, so build_schema.py
# projects them into a 1.6 KB file that ships. It changes when a default changes and never
# when a comment does, so both properties hold: one authored home, and no image churn from
# editing prose.
app/keys.json
app/workflow.schema.json
